From ce5828405d45492286110894e21360d1e311d2ef Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: pugant <pugant@localhost>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 19:01:46 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 02/15] llama-context: k-slot graph-result cache keyed by the
 reuse signature [w6-9 a2-c2]

The MTP round alternates graph keys within one context (verify n=d+1
all-outputs -> plain n=1 on the target; process -> step on the drafter),
so the legacy single gf_res_prev slot missed almost every eval (S0-c:
4-4.5 rebuilds / 7.7 evals per round at ~1.74 ms reset+build each).

Replace gf_res_prev with a small LRU cache of llm_graph_result slots
(default k=3, LLAMA_GRAPH_CACHE_SLOTS, 0/1 = legacy single-slot behavior
for same-binary A/B and runtime rollback; clamped to 8). The full
can_reuse predicate stays the single source of truth - the slot lookup
only routes the request. Exactly one slot can be "current" (the one the
scheduler has allocated): a hit on the current slot is the legacy fast
path, a hit on any other slot re-binds it with sched reset + alloc_graph
(compute_splits runs whatever the sched last allocated, and the cached
tensors' data pointers go stale once another graph is allocated on the
same sched), skipping only reset + build_graph. All external sched-reset
sites (sched_reserve, memory_update, graph_reserve, opt path) now
invalidate every slot.

New counters in perf_print_graph_build_data: cache hits, rebinds,
evictions, rebind timing. Gate N (bit-exact): logs/wave6/w6c9 harness,
CPU-only, 5-key MTP-shaped alternation + reserve/kv-clear invalidations
- output hashes identical k=0 vs k=2 vs k=3 across two seeds.

Co-Authored-By: GLM by z.ai <glm@z.ai>
